  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining Table Structure**\n\nI\'m currently working on enhancing the variance table\'s column structure within the `<thead>` element. Specifically, I\'m focusing on refining the table headers, starting with the \"Account / Category\" column. I\'m aiming for improved visual clarity and semantic accuracy within the header row.\n\n\n**Optimizing Table Rows**\n\nI\'m now shifting my attention to the table rows. I\'ve begun integrating the provided data within the `<tr>` elements. I\'m focusing on conditional formatting for the `Variance` column and aligning data appropriately. Additionally, I\'ve started the implementation for the \'Autonomous Root-Cause Attribution\' section within each row.\n\n\n**Implementing Data Rendering**\n\nI\'ve moved on to rendering the actual data now. I am working on integrating the data within the table cells, and also applying conditional styling to the variance amounts. Currently, I\'m formatting the numbers to ensure they\'re easily readable, using `toLocaleString()`, and making unfavorable variances stand out by using an amber color, and the favorable ones an emerald color.\n\nI\'m currently rendering account code and category, along with the actual, budget, and variance amounts, within their respective columns.\n\n\n**Analyzing Trend Visualization**\n\nI\'m now focused on the trend visualization. I\'m incorporating arrows, both up and down, to indicate variance percentage direction within a dedicated column. I am also working on adding percentage values alongside the arrow icons, clearly showing the variance. I\'m also attempting to create a visual indicator to show favorable trends versus previous periods.\n\n\n**Visualizing Trend Indicators**\n\nI\'m now implementing the trend visualization, and just added logic for conditional styling based on trend direction. I\'m using up/down arrows and percentage values, along with a \"favorable\" or \"unfavorable\" label, which appears visually in a new column. I\'ve set the favorable trends to be emerald, with an upward arrow. Unfavorable trends, by comparison, are amber, with a downward arrow.\n\n\n**Refining Prior Period Data**\n\nI\'m now integrating prior period data to provide context. I\'m focusing on incorporating the previous quarter\'s actual amount, labeled as \"Q2\", alongside the trend visualization. I\'ve successfully added the prior amount within the trend column, and am now adding the trend label alongside it to enhance readability.\n\n\n"
