    thinking: "**Designing the Preview Tables**\n\nI\'m now focused on designing the preview tables, placing them directly after line 1521, below the upload dropzones. My attention is on the component that will render the preview. I\'m thinking about the header elements: a title with a \"Table\" icon and the text \"Parsed CSV Structure Preview (First 5 Rows)\" and a subtitle that says \"Immediate schema\".\n\n\n**Refining Preview Details**\n\nI\'m now refining the preview table details. For the bank CSV preview, I\'m designing a card header with an appropriate icon, filename, row count badge, and a schema validation status badge. The table will have a header with row numbers and detected type pills for each column. I\'m focusing on clearly formatting row values, especially currency amounts and credit/debit indicators. I\'ve also added a view toggle that will allow side by side comparisons, or show each file independently. Finally, there will be a reset to default samples button to restore files after changes.\n\n\n**Crafting Detailed Footers**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the preview table footers. For both the bank and GL CSV previews, I\'m crafting informative footers. The bank CSV preview will have a footer stating \"Showing top 5 rows for immediate structure inspection. Matching algorithm will execute against all [total row count] rows.\" The GL CSV preview footer will state \"Showing top 5 rows for immediate structure inspection. General Ledger accounts mapped to trial balance.\" I\'m also ensuring the table gracefully handles dynamically generated columns and rows with fewer items. I\'m considering using helper functions within `src/App.tsx`. I will also check if a helper function `renderCsvTable` exists.\n\n\n**Evaluating Component Placement**\n\nI\'m now evaluating the best component placement, concluding that direct JSX or a dedicated subcomponent within `App` is ideal for clarity and state accessibility. I\'m focusing on the layout to achieve this, specifically the \"Parsed CSV Preview Section\". I am ensuring the structure is clear and well-organized to enable a smooth, user-friendly data display experience.\n\n\n**Clarifying Immediate Feedback**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the design for instant structure feedback, refining the visual presentation. I\'m focusing on incorporating an animated pulse effect for visual emphasis, accompanied by a concise description of the feature\'s function. I\'m also adding a view mode selector, providing users with options for how the preview data is displayed, which I believe will provide additional utility.\n\n\n**Refining Table Button Styling**\n\nI\'m now refining the table button styling. I\'m focusing on `activePreviewType` to control visual states, adding conditional classes for background, text, and border colors to highlight the active preview. This includes distinct color palettes for \'bank\' and \'gl\' preview buttons and hover states. I\'m also addressing file and row count badges in the preview table.\n\n\n**Adding Preview Table Structure**\n\nI\'m now adding the structure for both the bank and GL preview tables. I\'ve designed the header rows with row indices, data type badges inferred from header names (like \'Date\', \'Numeric\', or \'Text\'). The tables will display up to five rows, with empty cells defaulting to \"-\". I\'ve included informative footers, indicating the number of displayed and total records, as well as the file name. Finally, I\'ve added a reset button for quick data reversion.\n\n\n"
