    thinking: "**Constructing the Preview Component**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got a specific location identified in the code to build the preview tables component. It\'s going right there between lines 1531 and 1533. This should make it easy to drop in the functionality. Next, I\'ll start focusing on the Quick Structure Preview functionality.\n\n\n**Refining the Table Layout**\n\nI am now focusing on the table\'s visual representation within the preview component. I\'m exploring different layout options using `flex` and `grid` to ensure the table is responsive and visually appealing. The goal is to provide a clean, easily readable structure preview, and I\'m currently using a border and background to differentiate. I want to highlight the first five rows for clarity.\n\n\n**Implementing Preview Rendering**\n\nI\'ve made progress on rendering the first five rows immediately after parsing to verify column headers, date formats, and debits/credits, all while ensuring the text remains legible. I am also working on creating side-by-side view functionality, and handling button state transitions as they are selected.\n\n\n**Designing the Component Buttons**\n\nI\'m now focused on the button component and its states, like `activePreviewType`. I want to create a clear visual distinction between selected and unselected options. The styles will apply conditional classes. I\'m using `emerald` and `cyan` as the colors for different states to make them visually distinct, which I\'ll apply to different preview types. I\'m also including the row count in the button label.\n\n\n**Defining the UI Elements**\n\nI\'m now integrating the reset button and the preview card structure. I\'m focusing on the component\'s responsive grid layout, especially how it adapts with different preview types. I\'ve designed the button\'s style and functionality, considering states and interactions. I\'m ensuring it\'s clear and intuitive for users.\n\n\n**Refining the UI Display**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the UI display, specifically the appearance of the file name, the file type, and the row count within the preview card\'s header. I am using `flex` items, and utilizing the available space for an improved visual presentation. The text truncation and visual indicators of the row count and the file type are important.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Table Headers**\n\nI\'m now diving into the table header rendering. I\'m focusing on crafting the `<thead>` element. The goal is a clear, concise display of column headers, along with contextual hints about their data types. I\'m using inline styling for the headers to ensure clarity, adding data type hints for better user understanding. The header row should be visually distinct to enhance usability, and I\'m adding a `text-slate-500` color to the numbers.\n\n\n**Implementing the Row Logic**\n\nI am now implementing the logic for rendering table rows, starting with an edge case: displaying a message when no rows are found in the CSV. I\'m focusing on the rendering of individual table rows, and implementing the highlighting of each row on hover. I am now working on handling the scenario where a row\'s column is empty by using a nullish coalescing operator to display empty strings. I\'m also addressing the display of numbers, and handling the amount column.\n\n\n**Formatting the Amount Column**\n\nI\'m now focusing on formatting the amount column for better readability. I\'m dynamically applying styles based on whether a cell contains \"credit\" or starts with \"+\". Credits get an `emerald` color, while debits get the default color. I\'m using `tabular-nums` for consistent number display and nullish coalescing for handling empty cells to display a dash. I\'m fine-tuning the visual cues to distinguish credits and debits effectively.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Preview Table**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the table, including the footer to display the number of rows being previewed, and the number of columns matched, which I\'m displaying using green text. Now I\'m shifting to ensuring this logic correctly applies.\n\n\n**Revising the Header Elements**\n\nI\'m revisiting the preview header. I\'m focusing on the file details display, specifically the filename, file type, and total row count. I\'m utilizing `flexbox` for alignment. The goal is an aesthetically pleasing layout that is also easy to read. I\'m employing truncation to handle lengthy filenames and including visual cues for the file type. I will also incorporate the row count.\n\n\n**Refining the Table Header**\n\nI\'m now revising the table headers to be more informative. I\'m dynamically including data type hints below each header label, such as \"Decimal (USD)\" or \"ISO Date,\" based on keywords in the column name. I\'m using `toLowerCase()` to handle variations and make the hints contextually relevant for the user, and the hint color is muted. I\'m focusing on providing more context within the table.\n\n\n**Enhancing Header Clarity**\n\nI\'m now refining the table header\'s contextual hints to give the user better context. Using `toLowerCase()` on the column names, I\'m dynamically applying data type hints such as \"ISO Date\" or \"Chart of Accts\" to their associated column.\n\n\n**Improving Row Styling**\n\nI am now improving the styling of table rows. I\'m focusing on making it easier to read by adding a visual distinction for debit and credit amounts within each row. I\'m applying conditional styling based on the column name and value, making it easier to quickly identify the transaction type.\n\n\n**Improving Row Display**\n\nI\'m now refining the table rows, especially the display of financial data. I\'m leveraging conditional styling to visually differentiate credits, accounts, and other data types, and also implementing tabular figures. I\'m also addressing the issue of empty cells, and replacing the cells with dashes. Finally, I\'m working on the footer.\n\n\n\n"
