  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Access Control**\n\nI see a clear shift in how access control works. Previously, the `canApprove` value was determined locally, based on role. Now, with the introduction of `AuthContext` and `useAuth()`, I\'m getting a handle on the authenticated user data, specifically the `user` object. This contains important information like their name and role. It\'s a significant change.\n\n\n**Refining User Permissions**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into how roles and permissions are dynamically managed using `AuthContext`. I\'m especially focused on `requireController()`, which seems to be the central gatekeeper. The idea is to display a `Global Access Denied Security Modal` for certain actions if the user isn\'t authorized, along with an easy role-switching option. I also want to incorporate visual cues, like lock icons, to clearly indicate restricted actions, as well as authorization banners for specific views.\n\n\n**Visualizing User Interface**\n\nI\'m now thinking about how to visually represent the user\'s role and authorization status within the UI. I\'m considering a prominent header displaying the user\'s profile with a role badge and a role selector, accessible via an avatar or initials. I also plan to add an interactive tour button in the header. The UI should also feature lock icons for restricted actions.\n\n\n**Examining App Structure**\n\nI\'m now checking `src/App.tsx`. I\'ve confirmed `App` is the top-level component and plan to wrap `AppContent` with `AuthProvider`. This allows for direct use of `useAuth()` within the contained components, which is a standard pattern. I\'m also ensuring the current role is correctly defined. Furthermore, the `OnboardingTour.tsx` state machine smoothly transitions, automatically changing the active tab and highlighting the relevant components.\n\n\n**Implementing Role Checks**\n\nI\'m now integrating the role checks into the UI\'s functionality. The `currentRole` state, initialized to \'controller\', plays a key role, alongside the `demoStep` state. Helper functions like `canApprove` and `canReview` define permissions, which determine access. Specifically, I\'m integrating these role checks within handlers, such as `handleResolveException`, `handleRejectException`, `handleApproveAllExceptions`, and `handleRollback`, utilizing `requireController` to enforce authorization.\n\n\n**Integrating Security Checks**\n\nI\'m now focusing on intercepting unauthorized actions. If a `reviewer` or `read_only` user tries to approve, `requireController` will trigger a security modal. Also, I\'ve adjusted the tour state to use `isTourOpen` and implemented a guided demo button with an animated sparkle icon, and I\'m rendering `OnboardingTour` at the end of `AppContent`.\n\n\n"
