  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Project\'s Scope**\n\nI\'m starting by carefully examining the provided requirements for FrogeClose. I\'m focusing on understanding the scope and outlining a strategic approach to this project. I\'m taking care to analyze the existing code base, as specified in the prompt.\n\n\n**Defining the Technical Approach**\n\nI\'ve decided to keep the dark UI and am building a list to track development stages: done, partial, or not started. My plan is to build the project sequentially within this session. I\'m focusing on \"Prototype on synthetic data\" with AI Studio limitations, implementing interfaces where needed to demonstrate functionality. I\'ll clearly indicate such cases and provide production outputs like schema.sql and config. Also, I\'m now committed to strict TypeScript, input validation, centralized error handling, environment variables for secrets, and ensuring ALL security checks occur server-side. I will write automated tests as indicated, and I\'ll display the results.\n\n1.  AUTH AND TENANCY\n2.  DATABASE (schema.sql)\n3.  FILES (upload with limits, checksums, signed-url access, PII redaction, sample downloads)\n4.  BACKGROUND JOBS (async closes with states, retries, idempotency key, progress polling)\n5.  AGENT STATE\n\n\n**Developing the Machine**\n\nI am now structuring the \"MACHINE\" module, detailing steps for plan, ingest, reconcile, draft, verify, exceptions, and finalize functions. The deterministic engine will be implemented using plain code, for reconciliation using hash-join and bipartite matching, with support for ASC 606 waterfall, ARR calculation (MRR x 12), and ASC 340-40 capitalization/amortization. This is being structured with five real pass/fail invariants. I\'m focusing on exception handling, audit trails, stripe integration, security measures, cost control and observability and evaluation to measure progress.\n\n\n"
